WHAT: Removes InlineHint::Never from recv handler functions. When handlers
are inlined into the dispatch function, the optimizer can see through the
call boundary and eliminate redundant object allocations.

WHY: Each contract function was lowered as a separate handler function that
the dispatch function called. The Never-inline hint prevented merging them.
Once inlined, struct parameters that were allocated as objrefs in the handler
become visible to the caller's optimization scope, enabling scalarization
and dead store elimination.

GAS IMPACT: -1268 gas total. ALL 18 non-merkle functions improved.
  math: -121 to -124 each (-2.0%)
  amm/swapAForB: -124
  erc20: -68 to -136
  governance: -22 to -71
  escrow: -35 to -53
  amm: -24 to -55
  verifier/groth16Verify: -35

CODEGEN IMPACT: Function count in IR should drop as handlers merge into
dispatch. Object allocations may decrease as cross-function scalarization
becomes possible.

TEAM NOTES: This is the counterpart to step 19 (dispatch arm inlining).
Step 19 removed the hint but didn't improve gas; step 21 removed the
HANDLER hint and got real improvement. The distinction: dispatch arms
are thin wrappers, but handlers contain the actual function body. Inlining
the handler is what exposes struct allocation/access to the optimizer.
